    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Arthashastra is a compilation of earlier treatises, revised over centuries, which covers government, law, taxes, trade, spies, diplomacy and war.</li><li>Tradition ascribes it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, counsellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, but studies since 1965 have mostly rejected this, pointing to several authors and to details that fit the first centuries CE.</li><li>Thought lost by colonial-era scholars, it was identified in 1905 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> Oriental Library and published by R. Shamasastry in 1909.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The text advises that in areas struck by famine, epidemic or war the king should start public projects such as irrigation works and forts, and exempt those affected from taxes. It also recommends feeding horses and elephants that are too old, sick or injured to work.</p></aside>
    </section>

